-// Starts a message by archive ID or inline pointer and resets the current page.
-// With no loaded archive, an archive ID leaves the old page and pointer in draw-message state.
-void HuWinMesSet(HUWINID winId, u32 messNum)
+/* "Message Dir Over" crash fix. HuWinMesSet/HuWinInsertMesSet's "is
+ * messNum a real pointer, or a packed dir<<16|num value?" check was
+ * `messNum & 0x80000000` -- reliable on real hardware, where EVERY live
+ * pointer (static .bss/.data OR heap) unconditionally starts at MEM1's
+ * 0x80000000 base. This port's low-4GB game arena (src/os/arena.c)
+ * deliberately starts at 0x80000000 too, for this exact reason -- so HEAP
+ * pointers still satisfy the check -- but the executable's own fixed,
+ * non-ASLR image base (tools/build.py's IMAGE_BASE=0x10000000) does NOT,
+ * so a STATIC/GLOBAL (.bss/.data) buffer address passed this way has bit
+ * 31 CLEAR and gets misclassified as a small packed dir/num value
+ * instead. Confirmed by direct reproduction: REL/fileseldll/filesel.c's
+ * fn_1_36C4 passes the address of a static per-save-slot text buffer
+ * (living in the image's low .bss, around 0x10xxxxxx, not 0x80000000+) to
+ * HuWinInsertMesSet; the low bit pattern of THAT address decodes as a
+ * huge, bogus "directory" once right-shifted 16, printing "Error: Message
+ * Dir Over" and then (since neither original error path actually
+ * returns) chasing a wild offset computed from it -- a real access
+ * violation. Real dir/num encodings are always tiny (dir<<16|num; the
+ * largest dir this whole codebase ever uses is under 0x100) --
+ * comfortably below EVERY real pointer this port ever produces, whether
+ * image-based (0x10000000+) or arena-based (0x80000000+ and up) -- so a
+ * generous, safely-in-between threshold replaces the bit-31 check with
+ * one that is still correct for real hardware's own address range AND
+ * this port's. */
+#define MP6_MESSNUM_ENCODED_MAX 0x01000000u
+
+static void mp6_window_finish_message(HUWINID winId, u32 messNum)
@@ -1613,13 +1672,0 @@
